# Migrations target a hosted production database

The production database is a **hosted Supabase project**, not the local Docker instance in
`supabase/config.toml`. A migration file existing in this folder does not mean it has been
applied anywhere.

## Before writing a migration

Run `npm run db:status` to see what production already has. Never infer applied state from
the filenames in this directory.

## Rules

- Forward-only. Never edit or delete a migration that is already applied to production —
  write a new one instead.
- Filename format is `<UTC timestamp>_<snake_case_description>.sql`, matching the existing
  files. Ordering is by timestamp, so a new file must sort after every applied one.
- Destructive statements (`drop table`, `drop column`, `truncate`) need explicit user
  confirmation, because production holds real data.
- Every new table needs RLS enabled plus policies. Roles come from `app_metadata`, not
  `user_metadata`.
- `supabase/seed.sql` runs on local `db reset` only. Production must never depend on it.

## Verify locally, then promote

1. `npm run db:reset` — local only, rebuilds from all migrations plus seed
2. `npm test`
3. `npm run db:push` — applies to production
4. Only then push the code that depends on the new schema

Deploying code ahead of its migration produces a green Vercel build that fails at runtime.
